<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    ==============================================================================================
    Directory.Build.props
    The single shared build contract for every project in the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition.
    ==============================================================================================

    WHAT THIS FILE IS
    MSBuild imports this file automatically into every project beneath it by directory walk up, so
    it is the one place the build contract is expressed. All sixteen projects inherit it: the six
    shared libraries under shared/, their six sibling test projects, the four service applications
    under services/, and the four service test projects. No project restates any setting from here.

    EXACTLY ONE COPY MAY EXIST, AND IT LIVES HERE AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT
    A project under services/ therefore receives identical settings with zero duplication, which is
    what lets each service restore, build and test independently from a clean checkout while being
    unable to drift from the other three. Do NOT create a per service or per tree copy: MSBuild
    stops walking up at the FIRST Directory.Build.props it finds, so a second copy under services/
    or shared/ would silently shadow this one and reintroduce precisely the drift this file exists
    to prevent. A copy under a single service directory is the most damaging variant of that
    mistake, because the other three services would keep passing and nothing would report it.

    ORDER OF AUTHORSHIP, AND WHY IT MATTERS
        global.json                selects the SDK that evaluates this file
        Directory.Build.props      this file, which turns central package management ON
        Directory.Packages.props   inert until this file does so, then supplies every version

    TOOLCHAIN THIS CONTRACT IS VALIDATED AGAINST
        .NET SDK   10.0.302, pinned by global.json at this same repository root
        Runtimes   Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.0.10 and Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.10
        Language   C# 14

    PROVENANCE: THERE IS NO LEGACY SOURCE EQUIVALENT TO TRANSLATE
    The Appeon PowerBuilder 2021 framework this .NET tree is being added to has no build system in
    the MSBuild sense. Its nearest analogues are the two PowerBuilder project objects, and nothing
    here is a translation of either, because neither is usable:

        ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj   29 PBD: entries, COM:Appeon,       CPY: empty
        ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/p_pfw.srj     28 PBD: entries, COM:Sybase, Inc., CPY: empty

    They disagree on library count, they disagree on whether pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl participates at
    all, they disagree on the vendor, p_pfw.srj embeds developer workstation absolute paths under
    F:\pfw\ together with the retired library name pfwUtils.pbl, and BOTH declare
    pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which does not exist anywhere in the repository. Neither would build
    as written, so both are read as REFERENCE for build INTENT only. Their empty CPY: field, and
    their COM: values naming the PowerBuilder IDE vendor rather than the framework author, are also
    why the assembly metadata group below takes its holder from the root LICENSE instead.

    THE LEGACY TREE IS READ ONLY AND SHARES THIS WORKING DIRECTORY
    ws_objects/, oldversion/125/, pack/, res/, samples/, sciter_control/, the pre-existing browser
    harness directories under tests/, every *.pbl, *.pbt, *.pbw, *.pbr and *.pbd, and the closed
    source native Windows *.dll binaries all sit in this same checkout. They are the behavioural
    oracle for parity testing and are never a build input. This file therefore declares NO ItemGroup
    of any kind, so nothing here can pull a legacy path into a build, and the C# projects that
    inherit these settings live only under shared/ and services/, where the SDK's default source
    globbing cannot reach any legacy path.
  -->

  <!--
    FRAMEWORK AND LANGUAGE
    net10.0 is the validated target framework; nothing in this repository multi targets, so the
    singular TargetFramework is correct rather than TargetFrameworks.

    LangVersion latest resolves to C# 14 on this SDK. It is safe to leave floating precisely because
    global.json pins the SDK with rollForward latestFeature, which confines roll forward to 10.0
    feature bands and so cannot silently cross a major language version.

    Nullable and ImplicitUsings are set unconditionally here, applications and test projects alike.
    Nullable reference types are part of the enterprise baseline for this refactor and are not a per
    project preference: the ported return code algebra is explicitly tri state and depends on null
    being representable and visible, so a project that opted out would lose the compiler's help on
    the single most defect prone translation in the port.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WARNING POLICY: WARNINGS ARE ERRORS EVERYWHERE, TEST PROJECTS INCLUDED
    This is a quality gate, not a preference, and it has three consequences worth stating because
    each one surfaces somewhere else in the tree.

    1. It is what makes the NuGet dependency audit FATAL rather than advisory. The stock web
       template on net10.0 otherwise emits NU1903 for a known high severity advisory reached
       transitively. The remedy is the pair of mandatory pins in Directory.Packages.props, not a
       suppression here; this property is only what stops the finding from being ignorable.

    2. It applies to analyzer diagnostics shipped with packages as well as to compiler diagnostics.
       Concretely, the xunit.v3 analyzer diagnostic raised when an awaited call that accepts a
       cancellation token is not given the ambient test cancellation token is an ERROR in this
       repository. Test authors must pass it rather than expect a warning.

       Its boundary is worth stating precisely, because a green build does NOT mean the log is
       empty. This property becomes the compiler switch warnaserror+, so it governs compiler and
       analyzer diagnostics only. MSBuild TASK warnings are a separate channel and stay warnings:
       MSB9008, raised when a ProjectReference names a project file that does not exist, is the one
       to expect while the tree is still being filled in. Read the build log rather than trusting
       the exit code alone, and resolve an MSB9008 by creating the referenced project or correcting
       the reference. Promoting that channel from here is deliberately not done; see the closing
       section of this file.

    3. It is exactly why the repository root .editorconfig carries SCOPED naming analyzer
       suppressions. This refactor deliberately preserves the legacy SCREAMING_SNAKE constant
       identifier spellings verbatim, for example RetCode.OK, RetCode.E_INVALID_ARGUMENT,
       RetCode.CANCELLED, Enums.SQL_MS_REPLACE, Enums.INIT_FLAG_ENABLE_SQLITE and
       Categories.CAT_DWSVC, because those exact spellings appear in serialized payloads, in log
       records and in characterization recordings, where a rename would silently invalidate every
       stored comparison. Under this property the naming analyzer would turn each of them into a
       build error, so the suppressions are file glob scoped in .editorconfig to the files that
       genuinely carry those identifiers. They are deliberately NOT applied globally, and nothing is
       suppressed from this file.

    EnableNETAnalyzers and AnalysisLevel state the .NET SDK's own defaults for net10.0 explicitly
    rather than adding anything: the .NET analyzers are already enabled by default for net5.0 and
    later, and they are the analyzers the SDK itself carries, so no analyzer package is referenced
    to obtain them. They are declared because a warnings as errors gate is only reproducible if the
    diagnostic set producing those errors is stated rather than inferred, and because every project
    file in the tree documents both as inherited from here. As with LangVersion above, latest is
    bounded by the global.json SDK pin, so the rule set cannot shift under the gate: on this SDK it
    resolves to the analysis level 10 default rule set. No analyzer package, source generator, style
    enforcement package or code metrics setting is referenced anywhere in this refactor.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CENTRAL PACKAGE MANAGEMENT
    This switch is what activates Directory.Packages.props, the sibling file at this same repository
    root, which is inert without it. Every PackageReference in every project must therefore be
    VERSIONLESS; a project that carries an inline Version attribute fails the build with NU1008
    rather than quietly winning, and that failure is the desired behaviour.

    This is mandatory rather than merely convenient. It is the mechanism that forces the two
    mandatory security pins onto all four services from exactly one place, so no service can drift
    onto a vulnerable transitive version while every other service's audit still passes:

        Microsoft.OpenApi                 pinned to clear the transitive advisory while staying
                                          compatible with the OpenAPI source generator in this SDK,
                                          which is compiled against the 2.x object model
        SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3     pinned to override the vulnerable native bundle reached
                                          transitively through the EF Core SQLite provider

    The exact versions, the advisory identifiers and the compiler incompatibility that rules out the
    obvious forward move all live in Directory.Packages.props next to the pins themselves. Do not
    restate a version here; there is deliberately no PackageVersion or PackageReference item in this
    file.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ASSEMBLY METADATA
    Stated once here so that all sixteen assemblies agree, and taken from the root LICENSE rather
    than invented. The holder string and the 2013 - 2022 span are copied verbatim from LICENSE and
    cross checked against the license section of README.md, which reproduces the identical BSD
    2-Clause notice plus a four condition Chinese restatement. Do not substitute a different holder,
    a different span, or a transliteration: the legacy PowerBuilder project objects carry an empty
    copyright field and name the IDE vendor, so LICENSE is the only authoritative source.

    The copyright value deliberately names BOTH LICENSE and NOTICE. BSD 2-Clause requires the
    notice, the conditions and the disclaimer to be reproduced in the materials provided with a
    binary distribution, a container image is a binary distribution, and NOTICE additionally carries
    the four condition restatement and the upstream attributions the framework depends on. The root
    .dockerignore keeps both files in the build context on the strength of this metadata, so an
    image that dropped either one would be pointing at material it does not carry.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Product>PowerFramework</Product>
    <Company>金千枝(深圳)软件技术有限公司</Company>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2013 - 2022, 金千枝(深圳)软件技术有限公司. BSD 2-Clause. See LICENSE and NOTICE.</Copyright>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    DETERMINISM
    Deterministic compilation means identical inputs produce byte identical output, which is what
    makes a locally built assembly comparable with the one CI produces. ContinuousIntegrationBuild
    additionally normalizes the source paths embedded in the assembly, and it is applied only when
    the CI environment variable is set, which GitHub Actions does, so that a local build keeps the
    absolute paths a debugger needs.

    Kept deliberately minimal: no source link, no symbol publishing and no packaging properties,
    because nothing in this refactor requires them.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, EACH FOR A STATED REASON
    Recorded so the omissions read as decisions rather than oversights, and so nobody "completes"
    this file by adding one of them.

      * OutputType. It is a per project concern and setting it globally would break the tree in
        both directions: the four service projects are web applications, the twelve test projects
        must each be an executable because that is how xunit.v3 runs, and the six shared libraries
        must stay libraries.

      * IsPackable, GeneratePackageOnBuild, PackageId, and every other NuGet packaging property.
        Nothing here publishes a package. The shipped artifacts are four container images, one per
        in scope service.

      * InvariantGlobalization. The port carries a localization surface across en, zh-Hans and
        zh-Hant plus culture sensitive date and number formatting parity, so ICU must stay live.
        Trimming it would change observable behaviour, which this refactor forbids.

      * NoWarn, WarningsNotAsErrors, and any project wide or repository wide analyzer suppression.
        The only suppressions this refactor needs are the naming ones for the preserved constant
        identifiers, and they belong in .editorconfig scoped to the files that carry them. A
        suppression here would silently widen that scope to all sixteen projects.

      * MSBuildTreatWarningsAsErrors, which would promote the MSBuild task warning channel described
        in the warning policy section above. It is not set for two reasons. Nothing in this refactor
        asks for it, so adding it would be scope creep on a file whose whole purpose is to remove
        variation. And it would convert every MSB9008 into a hard failure, which turns the ordinary
        state of a partially populated tree into a build break and would make the failure look like
        a settings defect rather than the missing project it actually is. The compiler and analyzer
        gate above is the one this refactor requires; MSBuild task warnings are surfaced by reading
        the log and by CI, not by escalation from here.

      * GenerateDocumentationFile. With warnings as errors above, a missing XML documentation
        comment becomes a build error across the whole tree, and no requirement asks for a
        documentation file.

      * Any Configuration default or configuration forcing. A bare test run builds Debug even
        immediately after a Release build; that nuance is documented in docs/BUILD.md and must not
        be papered over from here, because doing so would make the documented per service command
        behave differently from the same command run anywhere else.

      * Any solution filter mechanism. .NET 10 emits the XML .slnx solution format and legacy .slnf
        filters are incompatible with it, failing with MSB4014 during restore, build and test.
        Build scoping is done by the per service .slnx files together with this property sheet.

      * Any ItemGroup whatsoever, and in particular any Compile, Content, None, EmbeddedResource or
        Remove item naming a legacy path. See the read only note in the header: the legacy tree is
        the behavioural oracle, never a build input.
  -->

</Project>
